{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Under section 10, deceptive similarity depends on whether one trade mark so nearly resembles another that it is likely to deceive or cause confusion—not simply on whether it has a logo.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s10",
          "quote": "For the purposes of this Act, a trade mark is taken to be deceptively similar to another trade mark if it so nearly resembles that other trade mark that it is likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual directs examiners to assess the marks as wholes, considering how their similarities and differences affect an ordinary consumer’s impression or recollection.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/4~1",
          "quote": "In particular, in evaluating the likelihood of confusion, the marks must be judged as a whole:"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/4~1",
          "quote": "What needs to be assessed is the effect of the similarities and differences on the impression or recollection an ordinary consumer of the goods or services would have of the trade marks and whether this creates a real and tangible danger of confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "A logo can help distinguish marks: the Manual’s Combined Communications example records a finding of deceptive similarity for the plain-word mark but not for the logo mark, explaining that the word mark lacked an accompanying device to help purchasers distinguish it.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/8~3",
          "quote": "In the Opposed Word Trade Mark there is no accompanying device to aid potential purchasers in distinguishing between it and the Opponent’s Registered Trade Mark. Deception or confusion is therefore likely to arise out of the use of the Opposed Word Trade Mark (at [77])"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/8~3",
          "quote": "I therefore find that the Opposed Word Trade Mark is deceptively similar to the Opponent’s Registered Trade Mark but that the Opposed Logo Trade Mark is not deceptively similar to the Opponent’s Registered Trade Mark (at [78])"
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Having a logo does not necessarily prevent deceptive similarity: the Manual’s realcommercial example records that differences in font, colour, a house device and additional features did not overcome the danger created by the common essential feature.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/8~4",
          "quote": "Whilst there are differences in font, colour and the use and placement of the house device as well as the existence of additional features, those elements do not do enough…to avoid the real danger of deception or confusion created by the common essential feature (at [236])."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Conversely, sharing words or another element is not, by itself, sufficient for an examiner to conclude that the marks are deceptively similar.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/4~1",
          "quote": "While a real and tangible danger of confusion can arise if one mark is contained in another, the existence of a common element is not sufficient to conclude the marks are deceptively similar."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The Manual requires a real and tangible danger of confusion, rather than a mere possibility; confusion includes consumers reasonably wondering whether the goods or services come from the same source.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/2#1",
          "quote": "A mere possibility is not sufficient."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/2#1",
          "quote": "the trade mark is likely to cause confusion if there is a real likelihood that some people will wonder or be left in doubt about whether the two sets of products or the products and services in question come from the same source."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/4~1",
          "quote": "whether this creates a real and tangible danger of confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "The effect of having or lacking a logo must therefore be assessed in context, including how the marks will be used, how the goods or services will be bought and sold, and who is likely to acquire them.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/2#1",
          "quote": "In considering whether there is a likelihood of deception or confusion all surrounding circumstances have to be taken into consideration. These include the circumstances in which the marks will be used, the circumstances in which the goods and services will be bought and sold and the character of the probable acquirers of the goods and services."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    }
  ],
  "declined": false,
  "concepts_used": [
    "GC-0154",
    "GC-0004",
    "GC-0038",
    "GC-0010",
    "GC-0008",
    "GC-0011"
  ]
}
